Data Structures and Algorithms
Data Structures are ways to store and organize data. Algorithms are step-by-step instructions used to solve problems or complete tasks. 
Data structures and algorithms work together to help programs handle data efficiently.

Linear Data Structure
A Linear Data Structure is a structure where data is arranged one after another in a sequence. Each item has a specific position in the sequence.

A One Dimensional 
Array is a data structure used to store multiple elements. The elements are arranged in a single sequence, and each element can 
be accessed using its position or index.

Big O Notation
Big O Notation is used to describe how the amount of work an algorithm needs changes when the amount of data becomes larger.

Basic Searching Algorithms
Searching Algorithms are methods used to find a particular item in a group of data.
Two basic searching algorithms are Linear Search and Binary Search. They work differently depending on how the data is organized.

Linear Search
Linear Search checks the data one item at a time, starting from the first item. It continues until the required item is found or there are no more items to check.

Binary Search
Binary Search finds an item by repeatedly dividing the data into two parts. It checks the middle item and then decides whether to search the left or right half.


